# Dependency-closed release preparation

The private research archive stays intact. No publication, binding license choice,
contributor consent, or source-redistribution right is implied by integration.

The path classifier alone is not a usable public-export specification: blanket removal
of `docs/audits/` and `docs/reports/` removes task evidence and canonical proof sources.
The dependency planner supersedes those default exclusions **only as review candidates**.
It reads one exact committed tree, records blob identities and dependency reasons, and
retains the complete ledger and its registered proof/audit/certificate sources, active
route modules/shared theory, task dependencies and regressions, linked evidence,
platform tools, and all research inputs where runtime dependencies remain uncertain.
It distinguishes these requirements from nonessential historical paths. Retaining all
research inputs is a conservative contingency set, not a claim of minimal closure.

```sh
python3 tools/release_dependencies.py --ref HEAD --check --summary
python3 tools/release_dependencies.py --ref HEAD > /tmp/e99-release-plan.json
python3 tools/publication_inventory.py --ref HEAD --dependency-plan /tmp/e99-release-plan.json --summary
```

`--check` verifies dependency closure, **not rights clearance**. Every one of the task
cards has explicit evidence paths in the machine-readable plan. Missing sources,
broken internal references, and uncleared literature without a replacement are errors.
Third-party PDFs and `extracted/` content are never copied. The
[literature replacement map](literature-links.json) records bibliographic identities and
external publisher/author/institutional links. A link is not a redistribution license.

The [file-review template](release-reviews.json) starts with no approvals. Store actual
reviewer/owner approvals in a private file outside the proposed export, and supply it
with `--review-file <private-record.json>`. That record must name the exact commit.
Keeping approvals outside the exported tree avoids self-referential approval hashes
and prevents private reviewer records from entering an export. Before any
local reviewed export, reviewers must approve content, attribution, privacy, and
license for **each exact blob**, with an identified reviewer; changed bytes invalidate
approval. The owner must approve the destination/release and choose the applicable
root license and contributor terms. Essential historical evidence receives the same
review as new prose; a task citation never clears a report by itself.

For unshared structural QA, `--local-candidate <new-local-directory>` builds a
rights-blocked candidate without altering any approval. It runs the export validators
and cannot be represented as cleared for redistribution. No rights are inferred from
that copy.

After those decisions, `--review-file <private-record.json> --export-reviewed <new-local-directory>` copies approved files
from the recorded tree, rewrites bundled-literature links to bibliography links,
regenerates the artifact index over retained evidence, checks unchanged generated
state views, runs theorem-state validation, and validates every task dependency and
internal link against the actual export. `--validate-export <directory>` repeats that
check and rejects files outside the planned export plus its regenerated artifact
index. No command publishes, pushes, changes visibility, or rewrites Git history.
An export that fails validation remains an unshared failed candidate.

Canonical definitions, theorem IDs, edges, statuses and mathematical claims are
preserved. The artifact index is a view of retained artifacts and is regenerated; it
must not list absent archive files. Ordinary state views continue to derive from the
complete manifest. An actual export must also replay the full certificate suite before
publication. The original private hash-backed baseline is historical and is not an
export byte-identity claim.

The current dependency plan can be mechanically complete while release clearance is
false. Pending owner/file-review decisions are blockers to generating a redistributable
export, not permission to omit necessary proofs or ship unusable task cards. Use the
[publication audit](PUBLICATION_AUDIT.md) for the rights/privacy checklist.

## Validation boundaries

Candidates are created with a private `0700` root directory. Before writing, the builder
checks the exact commit, Git blob, mode, source hash and planned rewritten hash.
Git reads ignore local replacement references and recompute blob identities from bytes.
An object alias cannot reuse approval of different content. The
manifest's registered paths, artifact roots and exclusions are checked before any
generator runs; its index output must match the declared generated overlay. The
validator rejects symlinks, special files, unsafe paths, extra files, changed bytes and
changed permissions before generators inspect the tree. It recomputes artifact metadata
only after file-set, type, mode, hash and link checks pass; standalone metadata inspection
rechecks manifest paths before reading any index or report. It then runs the complete
theorem-state checker against the exported files. This is a static
local check; do not modify a candidate concurrently with validation.

The scanner handles inline links/images, reference definitions, balanced or escaped
parentheses and entity references. Top-level fenced examples, code spans, comments and the
repository's display-math delimiters supply no navigational links and are not rewritten.
Literal existing evidence paths in inline code remain conservative dependencies. The
scanner also checks raw HTML `href` and `src` dependencies. Bundled-literature references in
raw HTML must be converted to Markdown before export, so they cannot evade the
bibliography rewrite. The scanner is not a complete Markdown renderer; a new navigation
syntax needs a scanner regression before relying on it for release closure. Unknown binary inputs and unexpected
executable source files block export rather than disappearing from the evidence set.

Approval records reject duplicate paths, nonboolean owner approval and stale commits.
Missing approval fields, blank reviewer identities and changed blobs remain pending.
The plan's `pending_review_fields` lists outstanding requirements without publishing
reviewer identities. A present root license is only a mechanical gate; its scope and
authority require human review.

Retained generator programs execute during structural QA. Review the selected source
code or run it in filesystem/network isolation before using an untrusted revision;
path validation does not sandbox arbitrary programs.

An unshared file export has no Git history. To rehearse commands that require Git,
initialize a disposable **local copy** with a new initial commit, use `--base-ref HEAD`
for its first full verification, and keep it unshared. Validate the original file export
separately; adding `.git/` to it correctly fails the exact-file-set check. Historical
baseline commits are available in the private archive, not in a clean-history export.

## Prepare the private rights-and-privacy review inventory

```sh
python3 tools/release_review.py --ref HEAD --output /private/audit/file-review.json --summary
```

Use an existing private directory outside the export. This generated inventory retains
every path/blob, dependency reason and pending field while grouping exact source hashes,
file types and review areas. It omits reviewer identities and approval records. A group
is a review aid: identical bytes can have different provenance/attribution obligations,
and path-based groups do not establish authorship. Each required path/blob still needs
its own approvals, including inspection of rewritten/generated output. No file is
removed, approved or licensed by this command.
